{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And you shall charge him</b> concerning Israel: Know that they are troublesome and rebellious — on condition that you accept this upon yourself. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 27:19). And it is not correct in my eyes, because Scripture says <b>before their eyes</b> [לעיניהם], for this matter is more fitting to be said between Moses and HaShem in private, since saying it in their presence would provoke insolence among them. And it is the way of moral instruction to warn him in their presence against their being troublesome and rebellious. Rather, <b>and you shall charge him before their eyes</b> — that he should charge him with the duties of the ruler and the judge, for since he is to be at their head, he should place them under his authority and warn him to exert himself greatly in their affairs, and to fight the wars of HaShem, and that he himself should be the one who brings them out and brings them in, and to be scrupulous in matters of justice. And this is fitting to be done before their eyes, so that they trust in him and obey him, for they will know that he conducts their affairs in the ways of truth, since so his master commanded him. And so Moses did, as it says there: \"And Moses called Joshua and said to him before all Israel, Be strong and courageous,\" and so on, until \"you shall not fear and shall not be dismayed\" (Deuteronomy 31:7–8). And in the Sifre: \"Charge Joshua\" — concerning the matter of Torah study; \"and strengthen him and give him courage\" — this teaches that there are not two leaders for a generation; and these are the duties of the ruler, that he should be firm and not fear the people. And in my opinion, according to the plain sense of Scripture, the reason for <b>and you shall charge him before their eyes</b> is the matter of appointment — that he should appoint him in their presence to be ruler over them, in the language of \"from the day that he charged me to be\" (Nehemiah 5:14), and likewise \"and YHWH charged him to be ruler over His people\" (I Samuel 13:14), and likewise \"from the day that I appointed judges over My people Israel\" (ibid. 7:11) — I appointed."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {0 "In the citation from I Samuel, Ramban gives two consecutive references: 'ויצוהו ה׳ לנגיד על עמו' (I Samuel 13:14) and 'למן היום אשר צויתי שופטים על עמי ישראל' (I Samuel 7:11); the latter citation is indicated only by verse numbers 'ז יד' in the Hebrew, here resolved to ibid. 7:11."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:09:25.710840644Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}